[package-declaration] Define platform-free package declaration (complete)
Files: proposals/target-factory.md; Cargo.toml; crates/**
Do: Specify package identity/version, assets, persistence/schema requirements, host-version compatibility, and render-vocabulary capability requirements without target declarations.
Accept: A package declares what it needs semantically; no package field names an OS, architecture, window system, GPU backend, or target triple; and the declaration can be consumed by nworlds test/run/package.
-> game-contract
> Package declarations express semantic requirements and compatibility, never OS, architecture, window-system, GPU-backend, or target-triple choices.
> Assets, persistence/schema, host-version, and render-vocabulary requirements remain package facts; target profiles remain host facts.
---
[render-batch] Define and implement minimal host-owned renderer-agnostic RenderBatch (complete)
Files: proposals/target-factory.md; proposals/rendering-contract.md; crates/engine-presentation/**; crates/engine-api/**; crates/caravan-demo/src/engine_integration.rs; crates/voxel-sample/src/engine_integration.rs; crates/nworlds-desktop/**
Do: Specify and implement the smallest host-owned draw vocabulary consumed by game render production from GameState + Tau. Define ownership, lifetime, Send/'static requirements, fire-and-forget submission, normalized clip-space geometry, and target RenderSink translation without importing wgpu or platform commands. Migrate the Caravan and voxel renderers and target sinks to the shared batch.
Accept: GameState + Tau is the only render-production input; RenderBatch is owned and disposable; no journal, worldline, Orchestrator, input, branch selector, device, or host-clock value can enter the batch; Caravan and voxel clients produce Frame<RenderBatch>; and target sinks consume the same batch without game-specific geometry types.
-> game-contract
> `GameState + Tau` is the complete render-production input; no journal, worldline, Orchestrator, input, branch selector, device, surface, or host-clock value enters `RenderBatch`.
> `RenderBatch` is an owned, disposable, `Send + 'static` value when the selected target queue requires it; a sink may copy, queue, submit, or discard it.
> The batch is an owned triangle-list of normalized clip-space positions and RGBA colors; target sinks do not interpret Caravan tiles, voxel blocks, cameras, or game state.
> Caravan and voxel renderers are clients that project into the batch; target execution remains below the game integration seam.
